## From design-taste

# Studio Design Taste

_Cross-project design taste. The Designer reads this every time, and it is
synced into each repo's Cursor rules. Teach it: "teach the designer: ..."._

## Principles
- Restraint over decoration. If it doesn't help the user act, cut it.
- Consistency beats cleverness — reuse patterns across apps.
- Generous whitespace. Let content breathe.
- Spend boldness in one place: one signature element carries the page;
  keep everything around it quiet and disciplined.
- Type carries personality. Pair a characterful display face (used with
  restraint) with a clean body face, on a real type scale.
- Avoid the AI-default looks unless the brief truly calls for one: cream +
  high-contrast serif + terracotta; near-black + one acid accent;
  hairline broadsheet with zero radius. They read as templated.

## Rules (learnings accumulate here — these also govern Cursor's code)
- Buttons: never pure black (#000). Use #111 minimum.
- Build surfaces from theme tokens (`bg-background`, `bg-card`,
  `text-foreground`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`), never
  ad-hoc hex.
- Max one accent color per screen (drive it through `--color-primary`).
- One density system per page (comfortable or compact). Consistent radius.
  Quiet, consistent icons (Lucide, h-4/h-5).
- Empty states always have one clear primary action.
- Never ship empty / loading / error states as raw placeholder text —
  design them (Skeleton, Alert, a designed empty card).
- Destructive confirmations use AlertDialog, never a plain Dialog.
- Don't nest cards inside cards inside cards; no gradients or
  glassmorphism on every surface.
- Prefer real components (Table, Tabs, Sheet, Dialog) over repeated
  `div rounded-xl border p-6` shells.

## From brand-voice

# Studio Brand Voice

_Cross-project voice. Brand/Copy reads this every time, and it is synced
into each repo's Cursor rules. Teach it: "teach brand: ..."._

## Voice
- Plain, human, and short. Write like you'd talk to a smart friend.
- Confident, not hype-y. No exclamation-mark spam, no "revolutionary".
- Active voice. Say what the user gets, not what the system does.
- Sentence case. No filler. Each element does exactly one job.

## Rules (learnings accumulate here — these also govern Cursor's code)
- Name things by what the user controls, never by how the system is built
  ("Notifications", not "Webhook config").
- Keep an action's name constant through a flow: a "Publish" button
  produces a "Published" toast — not "Submit" → "Success".
- Errors say what happened AND what to do next. Never apologize, never
  blame the user ("You entered it wrong" → "That code didn't match").
- An empty screen is an invitation to act — give it one clear action.
- A control says exactly what happens: "Save changes", not "Submit".

## From security-baseline

# Studio Security Baseline

_Non-negotiable security rules. The Cyberexpert reads this every time, and
it is synced into each repo's Cursor rules so Cursor codes to it by
default. Teach it: "teach the cyberexpert: ..."._

## Non-negotiables
- Secrets live in env vars, never in code, never in logs, never in the repo.
- Authorize every mutation on the server — never trust the client.
- Validate and size-limit all external input (bodies, uploads, params).
- Parameterized queries only. Never build SQL by string concatenation.
- HTTPS everywhere; secure, httpOnly cookies for sessions.
- Prefer a managed auth provider (Clerk / Auth0 / Descope) over hand-rolled
  JWT or session code.

## How the Cyberexpert reviews
- Default pass on every diff: the built-in `security-review` skill.
- High-stakes changes (auth, payments, PII, uploads): also run
  `/claude-security` → "scan changes" for a verified, panel-checked pass.

